Observation and analysis
Visually and functionally convergent at the centre: the Thousand-Armed Guan Yin is both the largest, most central, most elaborately framed figure and the deity for whom the temple is named. Toponym (南海觀音) matches central ICON without slippage — a clean case rather than a Tangkak-style anomaly. The interpretation rule "visual prominence does not determine status" is not in play here for the principal deity, because the community's naming of the temple independently confirms her status.
The rule does come into play, however, in the relation between Guan Yin and the background Buddha cohort. Visually, Guan Yin dominates; doctrinally, Buddhas surmount Bodhisattvas in the orthodox Mahayana cosmological hierarchy. Whether the background register marks this hierarchical surmounting iconographically (via clear elevation/recession) or leaves it implicit (via mere co-presence) is exactly the kind of question your grammar is built to ask, and it needs better-resolution photographs to answer.
The Thousand-Armed form itself is iconographically marked — it is a more esoteric/tantric form than the standard Guan Yin, associated with the Great Compassion Dharani (大悲咒) practice. Its selection over a simpler Guan Yin form raises the doctrinal register of the composition above what would be expected for a smaller community temple. If the five-coloured pillars and the 福德 reference are confirmed, you have a composition that mixes doctrinal-Buddhist centre with popular-religious peripheral signalling — a registral-syncretism case rather than an architectural-syncretism case like Shuang Lin.
